Add tests at the end:
```java
@Test
void trackedRunsSynchronouslyAndRecomputes() {
    WritableSignal<Integer> a = Signals.signal(1);
    List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();
    Tracked<Integer> tracked = Signals.tracked(a::get, values::add);
    assertEquals(1, tracked.initial());
    a.set(2);
    assertEquals(List.of(2), values);
    tracked.destroy();
    a.set(3);
    assertEquals(List.of(2), values);
}

@Test
void trackedNestedComputationDoesNotLeakReadsToParent() {
    WritableSignal<Integer> outer = Signals.signal(1);
    WritableSignal<Integer> inner = Signals.signal(10);
    AtomicInteger parentRuns = new AtomicInteger();
    AtomicInteger childRuns = new AtomicInteger();
    List<Integer> parentValues = new ArrayList<>();
    Tracked<Integer>[] child = new Tracked[1];
    Tracked<Integer> parent = Signals.tracked(() -> {
        parentRuns.incrementAndGet();
        child[0] = Signals.tracked(() -> { childRuns.incrementAndGet(); return inner.get(); }, v -> {});
        return outer.get() + child[0].initial();
    }, parentValues::add);
    assertEquals(11, parent.initial());
    inner.set(20);
    assertEquals(1, parentRuns.get(), "inner read belongs to the child computation");
    assertEquals(2, childRuns.get());
    outer.set(2);
    assertEquals(2, parentRuns.get());
}
```
But the nested tracked creation inside the parent supplier: `Signals.tracked` calls `untracked(computed::get)` which clears the parent context; child reads tracked by child. The child effect is created; its first run may be queued? The parent supplier runs inside the parent computed's context (during `untracked(computed::get)` at parent creation, the stack is cleared, then parent computed pushes itself). The child `Signals.tracked`'s `untracked` clears the stack, evaluates child computed, then creates child effect; `effect` flushes immediately (no flush in progress) and subscribes. Good.
- `inner.set(20)` → child effect re-runs, calls onRecompute (no-op). parentRuns unchanged. Good.
- But the parent supplier creates a **new child tracked** every parent re-run; the old child tracked is not destroyed (we don't destroy it in the test). For the test, outer.set triggers parent re-run, creating a new child tracked; old child still alive. childRuns increments. Fine.
- `Tracked<Integer>[] child = new Tracked[1];` generic array warning; use `AtomicReference<Tracked<Integer>>`. Let's use `AtomicReference`.
- Add import `java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference`.

Let's add these tests.

