`destroy()` was auto-merged to origin/main's version, which destroys all `bindings` (including slot tracked effects) but does not call `destroySlotTree`/slot bindings/inputs. Our branch's `destroy()` was changed to:
```java
public void destroy() {
    if (rootSlot != null) destroySlotTree(rootSlot);
    rootSlot = null;
    bindings.clear();
    nodeBindings.clear();
}
```
But the conflict resolution didn't touch destroy (auto-merged to HEAD's version? It seems HEAD's version remained because our branch's change maybe was identical? Wait, our branch had changed `destroy()`; origin/main also changed? The rebase auto-merged `destroy()` to origin/main's version (for loop). That means our slot tracked effects are in `bindings`, so `destroy()` destroys them, but it doesn't destroy slot bindings/inputs or set rootSlot null. It also destroys window metadata effects (good). But it leaves `slot.bindings`/inputs. We need to restore our `destroy()` semantics while keeping window metadata effects. Since `bindings` includes all slot tracked effects + node-level effects + window metadata effects, the loop destroys all. But `slot.bindings` lists still contain destroyed effects; `rootSlot` remains; not fatal but memory leak. Better to implement:
